                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input is 3x3, output 9x9, each cell of input corresponds to a 3x3 block. If input cell is red (2), copy the entire input pattern into that block? Wait let's check.\n\nVerifier: x0 = ofcolor(I, TWO) = indices of red cells. x1 = shape(I) = (3,3). x2 = multiply((3,3),(3,3)) = (9,9). x3 = canvas 9x9 zeros. x4 = rbind(multiply, (3,3)) so multiply each index by (3,3), i.e., block top-left corner. x6 = asobject(I) = all cells with values. x7 = shift(object, offset). x8 = mapply shift for each red cell position: shifts the entire input object by offset = red pos * 3. x9 = paint.\n\nSo the entire 3x3 input is placed (shifted) at each red cell's block position. Since input contains red cells themselves, and input object includes everything. So output has the whole input pattern (with reds) placed at block corresponding to each red cell.\n\nWait in the example, input:\nrow0: 0 0 0\nrow1: 0 2 2\nrow2: 0 0 0\n\nRed cells at (1,1) and (1,2). Multiply by 3: (3,3) and (3,6). Shift entire input object (containing 2s at (1,1),(1,2)) by (3,3) gives 2s at (4,4),(4,5). Shift by (3,6) gives 2s at (4,7),(4,8). Output row4: [0,0,0,0,2,2,0,2,2]. Yes matches.\n\nSo output = for each red cell position (r,c) in input, paste a copy of entire input grid at block (3r,3c).\n\nNow I need to create 3 demonstration pairs. Inputs must be 3x3 with at least one 2 presumably. Let's pick varied ones.\n\nPair 1: input with reds at multiple places, e.g.\n[2,0,0]\n[0,0,0]\n[0,0,2]\nRed at (0,0) and (2,2). Output: 9x9, paste input at block (0,0) -> 2 at (0,0); block (6,6) -> 2 at (6,6). Also input has other zeros. So output just two 2s. Not very informative.\n\nBetter to include other colors besides 0 and 2 to show full pattern copied.\n\nPair with colored pattern, e.g.:\n[1,2,0]\n[0,3,0]\n[4,0,2]\nRed at (0,1) and (2,2). Output: paste whole input at block (0,3) and block (6,6).\n\nLet's design: input:\nrow0: [1,2,0]\nrow1: [0,3,0]\nrow2: [4,0,2]\n\nShift by (0,3): cells: (0,3)=1,(0,4)=2,(1,4)=3,(2,3)=4,(2,5)=2.\nShift by (6,6): (6,6)=1,(6,7)=2,(7,7)=3,(8,6)=4,(8,8)=2.\n\nOutput 9x9 accordingly.\n\nAnother: input:\n[2,1,1]\n[1,2,1]\n[1,1,2]\nReds on diagonal, includes colors. Output copies at (0,0),(3,3),(6,6) blocks.\n\nThird: input:\n[0,0,2]\n[0,0,0]\n[2,0,0]\nSimple.\n\nLet's just provide inputs and let environment compute outputs. I'll pick 3.\n\nActually I should maybe make varied sizes/scenarios to teach. All inputs are 3x3 per task (verifier shape general mult but red positions...). Actually the rule says 3x3 input. Keep 3x3.\n\nLet me create:\n\n1. input:\n[[2,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,2]] \u2014 two reds. Simple but reveals blocks.\n\nHmm, better to show rule clearly: full pattern copy. Use non-zero non-red colors.\n\n2. [[1,2,1],[2,1,2],[1,2,1]] \u2014 many reds.\n\n3. [[0,2,0],[0,0,0],[0,2,0]]\n\nLet me just call show_transformed_input for three inputs.\n\nI'll choose:\n\nA: [[1,2,0],[0,3,0],[4,0,2]]\nB: [[2,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,2]]\nC: [[0,0,2],[0,5,0],[6,0,0]]\n\nThese give good coverage. Let's call.",
